---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Control', 's-maxage=300, stale-while-revalidate=3600');
---

<Layout
  title="ChatGPT vs Perplexity: Which is Better for Research in 2026?"
  description="ChatGPT vs Perplexity compared for research, writing, coding, and everyday use. Verdict table, pricing, and which to choose in 2026."
>
  <div class="px-6 py-12 max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-8">

    <!-- H1 -->
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a]">ChatGPT vs Perplexity: which is better for research in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-base leading-relaxed">
        ChatGPT and Perplexity serve fundamentally different purposes — ChatGPT generates, Perplexity searches and synthesises with citations. For most tasks they're not really competing. But for research, the choice matters, and picking the wrong tool wastes time. Here's where each one wins.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2 #1: Quick verdict -->
    <div class="border border-[#c8c0a8] rounded-xl p-6 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a]">ChatGPT vs Perplexity: quick verdict</h2>
      <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-sm leading-relaxed">Perplexity wins on sourced research. ChatGPT wins on everything else.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-[#1a1a1a] text-white">
              <th class="text-left p-3 rounded-tl-lg">Use case</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3">Winner</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 rounded-tr-lg">Why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="bg-[#f5f0e8] border-b border-[#c8c0a8]">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1a1a1a]">Research</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold">Perplexity</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-[#6b6559]">Real-time sources with verifiable citations</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white border-b border-[#c8c0a8]">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1a1a1a]">Writing</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold">ChatGPT</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-[#6b6559]">Better creative range and tone control</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[#f5f0e8] border-b border-[#c8c0a8]">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1a1a1a]">Coding</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold">ChatGPT</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-[#6b6559]">GPT-4o significantly ahead on complex tasks</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium text-[#1a1a1a]">Everyday Q&A</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold">ChatGPT</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-[#6b6559]">More flexible, handles ambiguity better</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- H2 #2: What ChatGPT does better -->
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a]">What ChatGPT does better</h2>
      <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-sm leading-relaxed">
        ChatGPT is a generative AI — it writes, reasons, codes, and creates. Perplexity does not compete here. For creative writing, long-form content, copywriting, and anything requiring voice or tone, ChatGPT is the clear choice. Its ability to hold a long multi-turn conversation and iteratively refine output makes it the better tool for drafting, editing, and ideation workflows.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-sm leading-relaxed">
        For coding, ChatGPT (GPT-4o) is substantially stronger — it debugs, explains logic, writes tests, and refactors across dozens of languages. Perplexity can answer coding questions but it's searching for answers, not reasoning through them.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Image generation via DALL·E 3 and GPT-4o's native image model, custom GPTs, memory, and a broad plugin ecosystem are all ChatGPT exclusives. If your workflow goes beyond question-answering, ChatGPT is the more complete platform. Claude is the main alternative worth considering for long-document tasks — see ChatGPT vs Perplexity vs Claude for a three-way breakdown.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2 #3: What Perplexity does better -->
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a]">What Perplexity does better</h2>
      <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Perplexity's core advantage is verifiable, sourced answers in real time. Every response cites its sources with clickable URLs — you can check the primary source immediately, which ChatGPT's responses don't offer by default. For academic research, fact-checking, market data, current events, and any task where you need to trust the output, Perplexity's citation model is a genuine structural advantage.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Perplexity Pro ($20/month) unlocks deeper research modes, academic paper search, and access to multiple underlying models (GPT-4o, Claude, Gemini) within a single interface. Compared to ChatGPT Plus ($20/month), Perplexity Pro is the better value specifically if research with citations is your primary use case. For everything else — writing, coding, creative tasks — ChatGPT Plus wins the value comparison.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Where Perplexity consistently wins: looking up recent news, checking product specs, researching companies, finding academic papers, and verifying claims. Think of it as a smarter search engine, not a replacement for ChatGPT.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <div class="border border-[#c8c0a8] rounded-xl p-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-4">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] text-sm">Is Perplexity better than ChatGPT?</p>
          <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-xs mt-1">For sourced research that needs verifiable citations, yes. For writing, coding, creative tasks, and extended conversation, ChatGPT is better. They solve different problems — most power users end up using both.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] text-sm">ChatGPT vs Perplexity vs Gemini — which wins?</p>
          <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-xs mt-1">Perplexity for cited real-time research. <a href="/compare/gemini-vs-chatgpt" class="text-[#1a1a1a] underline hover:no-underline">Gemini</a> for structured output and Google Workspace tasks. ChatGPT for creative writing, coding, and general-purpose generation. All three have meaningful free tiers — try each on your actual workflow before committing to a paid plan.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] text-sm">Is Perplexity free compared to ChatGPT?</p>
          <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-xs mt-1">Both offer free tiers. Perplexity free includes real-time search with citations and limited Pro searches per day. ChatGPT free includes GPT-4o with usage limits. Paid plans are both $20/month — Perplexity Pro for research-heavy users, ChatGPT Plus for generative tasks.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <div class="border border-[#c8c0a8] rounded-xl p-6 text-center space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-[#1a1a1a]">Get more from ChatGPT with a structured prompt</h2>
      <p class="text-[#6b6559] text-sm">If ChatGPT is your choice, a structured prompt gets dramatically better results from every interaction.</p>
      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3 justify-center mt-2">
        <a href="/chatgpt-prompt-generator" class="py-3 px-6 bg-[#f0b429] border-2 border-[#1c1c1c] rounded-xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a] hover:brightness-95 transition-all text-sm">
          ChatGPT Prompt Generator →
        </a>
        <a href="/compare" class="py-3 px-6 bg-[#e4dfc8] border-2 border-[#1c1c1c] rounded-xl font-bold text-[#1a1a1a] hover:brightness-95 transition-all text-sm">
          More AI Comparisons →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</Layout>
